  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Dell Federal Systems L P recorded $1.2B in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date across 440 awards, with an average award size of $2.8M and obligations down 7.4% year over year. The Department of Veterans Affairs is the largest buyer, obligating $548M, followed by the Department of Homeland Security with $246M and the Department of Health and Human Services with $138M. Software Publishers is the dominant market, accounting for $990M and a second Software Publishers entry at $125M. Other notable markets include Other Computer Related Services at $58.9M and Electronic Computer Manufacturing at $53M. Historical fiscal-year obligations show $1.6B in FY21, $1.7B in FY22, $1.4B in FY23, $1.5B in FY24, and $1.3B in FY25. The Department of Defense obligated $132M in FY2026 to date, while smaller buyers include the Department of Commerce at $55.7M and the Department of Transportation at $51.4M. Current-year totals are partial and reflect contract obligations unless otherwise noted.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies drive Dell Federal Systems L P's federal revenue?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs is the single largest buyer, obligating $548M. The Department of Homeland Security and the Department of Health and Human Services follow with $246M and $138M respectively. The Department of Defense obligated $132M, and other buyers include the Department of Commerce at $55.7M and the Department of Transportation at $51.4M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which markets and product lines account for most obligations?",
        "body": "Software Publishers is the largest market, with $990M and a second Software Publishers line at $125M. Other Computer Related Services and Electronic Computer Manufacturing account for $58.9M and $53M respectively. Several smaller markets include Computer Storage Device Manufacturing at $4.6M and Computer and Computer Peripheral Equipment and Software Merchant Wholesalers at $743K."
      }
    ]
  },
